arXiv:2605.07105v1 [cs.LG] 08 May 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line" lang="en">
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="p1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\metadata</span>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p">[Correspondence]<span id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Lucas Monteiro Paes: <a href="mailto:lucasmp@apple.com" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">lucasmp@apple.com</a>; Natalie Mackraz: <a href="mailto:natalie_mackraz@apple.com" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">natalie_mackraz@apple.com</a></span></p>
</div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Lucas Monteiro Paes
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Natalie Mackraz
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Barry-John Theobald
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Federico Danieli
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Apple
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">August 24, 2026</span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Language model (LM) alignment improves model outputs to reflect human preferences while preserving the capabilities of the base model. The most common alignment approaches are (i) reinforcement learning, which maximizes the expected reward under a KL-divergence constraint, and (ii) best-of-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> alignment, which selects the highest-reward output among <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> independent samples. Despite their widespread use, the fundamental limits of reward improvement under a KL budget remain poorly understood.
We characterize the information-theoretic limits of KL-regularized alignment by deriving the maximum <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">achievable</em> expected reward gain for a fixed KL-divergence budget. Our first result provides a closed-form expression for the optimal reward improvement, governed by a Jeffreys divergence term rather than the <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\texttt{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\texttt{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> used in prior analyses. We further reformulate this expression as a covariance under the base model, yielding a practical estimator that predicts achievable alignment gains from base model samples alone.
We extend our analysis to the proxy reward setting, showing that the gap between ideal and proxy alignment (<em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reward hacking</em>) grows with the magnitude of reward error and when the KL penalty factor decreases.
We then prove that reward ensembling mitigates reward hacking, providing a theoretical justification for this technique used in practice.
Empirically, we compute the KL–reward Pareto frontier for two tasks for LMs, safety and summarization, and show that best-of-<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> closely approaches the theoretical limit, while PPO and GRPO remain substantially suboptimal.
Our theoretical results shed light on several empirically observed phenomena in the alignment literature and suggest that algorithmic improvements are needed to achieve optimal alignment without high inference costs.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) perform complex tasks on behalf of users: such as solving mathematical problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, moderating online content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, writing code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and shopping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.
While these LLMs solve multiple problems without fine-tuning, their output may be toxic, deceiving, or otherwise harmful <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
To ensure that models perform in a safe manner and avoid producing harmful outputs, they usually undergo an <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alignment</em> step to ensure their outputs follow human preferences that ideally reflect ethical values and social norms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Many approaches for aligning language models have been proposed including, (i) KL-regularized reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, (ii) direct preference optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, (iii) best-of-<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> finetuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, among others <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>.
At their core, these methods aim to solve a KL-regularized reinforcement learning (RL) problem shown in <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. This approach begins by defining a reward function <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> which scores model outputs according to how well they align with user preferences.
A base LLM <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> is then fine-tuned to maximize the expected reward while penalizing (proportionally to a parameter <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>) the KL-divergence from the base, leading to the aligned model <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{r,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the rapid proliferation of alignment algorithms, their theoretical limits remain largely unaddressed.
Progress in alignment is typically measured by achieving higher expected reward for a fixed KL divergence budget <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, where the KL divergence acts as a proxy for how much of the base model’s general capabilities we retain after alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
While alignment methods continuously push the empirical Pareto frontier outward, the true theoretical limit (the maximum achievable reward for a given KL budget) remains generally unknown—see <a href="#S2" title="2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for a discussion on the limits of alignment.
Without characterizing this fundamental limit, it is impossible to determine whether current techniques are approaching optimality.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07105v1/estimation_limit/fig1_legend.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/26;" width="476" height="26" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07105v1/estimation_limit/beavertails_fig1_nolegend.png" id="S1.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/314;" width="476" height="314" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Zephyr 7B with Beavertails</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07105v1/estimation_limit/smooth_tldr_no_label.png" id="S1.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/306;" width="476" height="306" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pythia 1B with TL;DR</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S1.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fundamental Limits of Alignment.</span> Reward gain vs. KL divergence from <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> for best-of-<math id="S1.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (BoN), GRPO, PPO, and the theoretical limit computed using <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (Monte Carlo Estimator for Δ(𝑟,𝑟^′)). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Def.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Monte Carlo Estimator for KL Divergences). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Def.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Each PPO/GRPO point corresponds to a checkpoint and each BoN point to a value of <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
In both cases, BoN closely track the performance of the theoretical limits, whereas GRPO and PPO remain sub-optimal. The x-axis goes up to the maximum KL measured by the theoretical limit, which varies across tasks.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fundamental Limits of Alignment.</span> We explicitly characterize the <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fundamental limit of language model alignment</em>, shown in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. First, we show that the reward gain after <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optimal alignment</em> is given by <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda\texttt{JD}(\pi_{\texttt{base}}||\pi_{r,\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">JD</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\texttt{JD}(\pi_{\texttt{base}}||\pi_{r,\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is the KL penalty used to restrict the KL divergence (see Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) and <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">JD</span> is the Jeffreys divergence, which is given by the sum of the forward and reverse KL divergence, i.e., <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\texttt{JD}(P||Q)\triangleq\texttt{KL}(P||Q)+\texttt{KL}(Q||P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">JD</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{JD}(P||Q)\triangleq\texttt{KL}(P||Q)+\texttt{KL}(Q||P)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
This improves upon previous work that provided only a <em id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-achievable</em> upper bound characterized by the square root of the KL divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. While our characterization is exact, the limit expression depends on the aligned model <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{r,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is available only after <em id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optimal alignment</em>—undermining its usefulness as a predictive tool.
We resolve this circularity by showing that the Jeffreys divergence term can be rewritten as a covariance under <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> alone, thus making it estimable before any post-training is performed.
This gives practitioners a principled way to measure how close their alignment method comes to the optimal trade-off.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proxy Rewards and Ensembling.</span> The limits of alignment relies on ideal (<em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gold</em>) reward models (<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>).
In practice, rewards are learned from human annotations and are noisy approximations, called <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proxy rewards</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> denoted by <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We extend our characterization to the proxy-reward setting and show that the gap in reward gain between alignment under the gold reward and under a proxy reward scales with the reward error <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}-r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}-r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and with the inverse of the KL penalty term, i.e., <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, quantifying how reward misspecification degrades alignment and <em id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explaining why aggressive alignment (smaller <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>) using a proxy reward leads to reward hacking as observed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. [19, Figure 1]</a></cite></em>.
We further leverage our framework to justify the effectiveness of <em id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reward ensembling</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> as a practice to mitigate hacking.
We show that, under mild assumptions, averaging the predictions of <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> reward models causes the reward of the resulting aligned model to converge to the ideal prediction at a rate of <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{-1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{-1/2})</annotation></semantics></math>, thus providing a theoretical justification for reward ensembling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Results.</span> We evaluate our fundamental limits in two settings: safety alignment using the BeaverTails dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> and summarization from human feedback using the TL;DR dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>.
First, we empirically validate the estimators derived from our framework, observing fast convergence when predicting the expected reward gain directly from the base model.
Second, we benchmark widely used alignment algorithms—specifically PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, and best-of-<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sampling—against the theoretical Pareto frontier.
Notably, our results demonstrate that <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">best-of-<math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> approaches the fundamental limit</span>, closely matching our derived optimal reward-KL Pareto frontier — <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions include:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fundamental limit of alignment.</span> We prove that the optimal reward gain after alignment is exactly <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda\,\texttt{JD}(\pi_{\texttt{base}}\|\pi_{r,\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">JD</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\,\texttt{JD}(\pi_{\texttt{base}}\|\pi_{r,\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">JD</span> is the Jeffreys divergence—<a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment). ‣ 3.1 Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Thm.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. This disproves the previously believed scaling of <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\texttt{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\texttt{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> empirically observed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and proved to be at least an upper bound for reward gain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fundamental limit estimator.</span> We show that the optimal reward gain after alignment can be rewritten as a covariance term under the base model alone, making it computable <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">before alignment</em>—<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Theorem 2 (Computable Limit of Alignment). ‣ 3.1 Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. We also derive an estimator of the KL divergence between the aligned model and the base model in <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2 (Convergence of KL Estimators). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, allowing us to estimate the reward–KL Pareto frontier.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why Overoptimization leads to Reward Hacking.</span> We characterize how proxy rewards degrade alignment, showing that the post-alignment reward deviates from ideal when reward error grows and the KL penalty decreases—<a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (The Impact of Proxy Rewards). ‣ 4.1 The Impact of Proxy Rewards in Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. This explains why aggressive alignment using a proxy reward leads to reward hacking, as observed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. [19, Figure 1]</a></cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why Reward Ensembling Improves Reward Hacking.</span> We leverage the covariance form to prove that reward ensembling mitigates reward hacking in <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Rate of Convergence for Reward Ensemble). ‣ 4.2 Why Reward Ensembling Mitigates Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Thm.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> as previously empirically observed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical verification &amp; Optimality of best-of-<math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> We validate our estimators and benchmark alignment methods against it (PPO, GRPO, best-of-<math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>), demonstrating that best-of-<math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> closely matches our derived theoretical reward—KL Pareto frontier (<a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Showing why best-of-<math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> was previously observed to be a strong baseline for language model alignment.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Language model.</span> A large language model (LLM) is a conditional probability distribution <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> over sequences of text <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a user query <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model generates a response <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> according to said distribution, <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{y}\sim\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\sim\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In practice, <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained through pretraining and supervised fine-tuning to follow user instructions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>.
This training procedure yields a highly capable model often referred to as a <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">base model</em>, but model responses may not align with human values and preferences, motivating the need for an alignment procedure to ensure safe model behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reward models.</span>
Several alignment procedures rely on a <em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reward model</em> <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> that assigns a scalar score <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math> measuring the quality of alignment of response <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> to query <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Ideally, the reward reflects user preferences: if a user prefers <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>w</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}^{w}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> in response to <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the associated reward should also be larger.
If a reward model perfectly reflects user preferences it is termed <em id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gold reward</em> and we denote it by <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Reward models are typically learned from preference data
 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. However, due to annotation noise and the stochastic nature of training a reward model, the resulting model is an imperfect proxy for the true user preferences, often called a <em id="S2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proxy reward</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, which we denote by <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We analyze the difference in models aligned with a gold and a proxy reward in <a href="#S4" title="4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">KL-regularized alignment.</span>
At the heart of several alignment techniques
 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>
lies the KL-regularized reinforcement learning problem <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.
Specifically, to nudge the base model <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> toward user preferences, these approaches seek an aligned policy over the vocabulary <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes the expected reward while penalizing deviation from the base model <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formally, this amounts to finding the aligned model <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{r,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> that optimizes the following objective:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{r,\lambda}\triangleq\arg\max_{\pi}\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{D}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}\sim\pi(\cdot|\mathbf{x})}\left[r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\right]-\lambda\texttt{KL}(\pi||\pi_{\texttt{base}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≜</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r,\lambda}\triangleq\arg\max_{\pi}\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{D}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}\sim\pi(\cdot|\mathbf{x})}\left[r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\right]-\lambda\texttt{KL}(\pi||\pi_{\texttt{base}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The KL penalty term in Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> prevents the model from drifting too far from <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> directly controlling the strength of this regularization.
In fact, we show in <a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (The Impact of Proxy Rewards). ‣ 4.1 The Impact of Proxy Rewards in Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> that smaller KL penalties are related to more reward hacking.
A fundamental property of Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> is that it admits a closed-form solution given by the following tilted distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{r,\lambda}(\mathbf{y}\mid\mathbf{x})=\frac{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\mathbf{y}\mid\mathbf{x})\,e^{r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}}{Z_{r,\lambda}(\mathbf{x})},\qquad Z_{r,\lambda}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}\sim\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}\left[e^{r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r,\lambda}(\mathbf{y}\mid\mathbf{x})=\frac{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\mathbf{y}\mid\mathbf{x})\,e^{r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}}{Z_{r,\lambda}(\mathbf{x})},\qquad Z_{r,\lambda}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}\sim\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}\left[e^{r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">partition function</em> and normalizes <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\mathbf{y}\mid\mathbf{x})\,e^{r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\mathbf{y}\mid\mathbf{x})\,e^{r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> into a probability measure <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{r,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use the result in Equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> to derive exact characterizations of the reward gain achievable under Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, and to study how proxy rewards and reward ensembling affect the said gain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limits of Alignment &amp; The Optimality of best-of-<math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Prior work suggested the optimality of best-of-<math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. However, existing justifications rely on structural assumptions on language and reward models.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. [52]</a></cite> showed that BoN converges to the optimal aligned policy as <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, but assumed linear reward models and memoryless language models.
Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gui et al. [20]</a></cite> studied the win-rate–KL Pareto frontier and found that best-of-<math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> approaches it.
However, their analysis focused on win-rate rather than reward maximization which is the optimization objective used in practice, and they also
assumed continuous language and reward models.
In contrast, we derive the exact reward–KL Pareto frontier for standard reward maximization without structural assumptions on the language or reward model, giving a direct way to check how close BoN follows the optimum at every value of <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, and in our experiments BoN closely tracks the entire frontier.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reward Ensembling.</span>
Reward ensembling has emerged as a practical strategy for mitigating reward hacking. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coste et al. [15]</a></cite> show that ensembles of reward models reduce reward hacking.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eisenstein et al. [17]</a></cite> compared different ensembling strategies showing that some are more effective than others on reducing hacking.
In contrast, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. [54]</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahmed et al. [1]</a></cite> focused on improving the efficiency of ensembling by building light-weight reward models for the ensemble.
While these works show empirically that reward ensembling mitigates reward hacking in practice, they do not provide a theoretical explanation for the observation, which we establish in <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Rate of Convergence for Reward Ensemble). ‣ 4.2 Why Reward Ensembling Mitigates Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Thm.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment.</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we compute the limit of reward gain for a KL-divergence penalty and provide an estimator to compute the limit with convergence guarantees.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous work has devoted interest to understanding the dependencies of reward improvement and the distance between the base and aligned models (measured as KL divergence).
Specifically, it has been empirically observed that <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}}[r]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}}[r]\approx\sqrt{\sigma\texttt{KL}(\pi_{r,\lambda}||\pi_{\texttt{base}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}}[r]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}}[r]\approx\sqrt{\sigma\texttt{KL}(\pi_{r,\lambda}||\pi_{\texttt{base}})}</annotation></semantics></math> for a constant <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mroueh and Nitsure [30]</a></cite> showed that, for a sub-gaussian reward, this empirically observed approximation was actually an upper bound to the reward gain after alignment, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}}[r]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}}[r]\leq\sqrt{\sigma\texttt{KL}(\pi_{r,\lambda}||\pi_{\texttt{base}})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}}[r]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}}[r]\leq\sqrt{\sigma\texttt{KL}(\pi_{r,\lambda}||\pi_{\texttt{base}})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The result from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> in Equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> has three main limitations: (i) it does not provide a closed-form expression for the limits of reward gain, only an upper-bound; (ii) it is not computable (we don’t have access to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{r,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>); (iii) it relies on the assumption that the reward distribution is sub-Gaussian.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">We move away from upper bounds and show a closed-form solution</em> for the improvement in reward after alignment. Specifically, we prove that the optimal reward improvement is completely characterized by the Jeffreys divergence, i.e., the sum of the forward and reverse KL divergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
If <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{r,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is the solution of Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> that aligns <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, then for every fixed query <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}\sim\pi_{r,\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]-\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}\sim\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]=\lambda\texttt{JD}(\pi_{\texttt{base}}||\pi_{r,\lambda})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">JD</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}\sim\pi_{r,\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]-\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}\sim\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]=\lambda\texttt{JD}(\pi_{\texttt{base}}||\pi_{r,\lambda}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment). ‣ 3.1 Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that the reward improvement after alignment is fully characterized by the Jeffreys divergence, the sum of the reverse and forward KL divergence, and not only the forward KL divergence as previously believed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.
Notably, our result does not impose any restrictions on the distribution of the reward or language model being used.
However, for fixed <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, it is not straightforward to compute the gain in reward because the computation of the Jeffreys divergence requires access to samples from the optimally aligned model <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{r,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, which <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">we do not have access to</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For generality, we measure performance after alignment in our theoretical analysis using a reward <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> that <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">may</em> be different from the reward we are aligning to <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
This generality is useful to analyze reward gain when we have access to only proxy rewards in <a href="#S4" title="4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
To this end, we denote the reward improvement measured with <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> after aligning the base model using a reward <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\Delta}(r,r^{\prime})\triangleq\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≜</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta}(r,r^{\prime})\triangleq\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we provide a characterization of reward gain after alignment that requires access to samples from the base policy <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> and scores from the reward model.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Computable Limit of Alignment)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the reward model with respect to which we are measuring performance after alignment using Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>—which may be equal or
different from the reward model we align the model to (<math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>)—then for every fixed query <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}{\Delta}(r,r^{\prime})=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]=\texttt{Cov}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}\left(r^{\prime},\frac{e^{r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}}{\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}\left[e^{r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}\right]}\right).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Cov</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}{\Delta}(r,r^{\prime})=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]=\texttt{Cov}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}\left(r^{\prime},\frac{e^{r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}}{\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}\left[e^{r(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}\right]}\right).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.E4" title="In Theorem 2 (Computable Limit of Alignment). ‣ 3.1 Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> shows that the expected gain in reward after alignment can be written as a function of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the reward model, not requiring samples from the optimally aligned model <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{r,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, the covariance computation requires access to all possible samples <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\sim\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\sim\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is infeasible due to the sampling cost and the number of potential responses being combinatorial. We next address this.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having established that the optimal expected reward gain can be exactly expressed as a covariance under the base model in <a href="#S3.E4" title="In Theorem 2 (Computable Limit of Alignment). ‣ 3.1 Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, we now operationalize this result.
The critical advantage of the covariance formulation is that it allows us to estimate information-theoretic optimal post-alignment performance without any training, using only generations from the unaligned base model.
We formalize the estimator for the optimal reward gain <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta}(r,r^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta}(r,r^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> Equation <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> next.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Monte Carlo Estimator for <math id="Thmdefinition1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta}(r,r^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta}(r,r^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{y}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{y}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples from <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the query <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> a gold reward model, <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> a proxy reward model, and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The Monte Carlo estimator for the expected gain in reward is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r^{\prime})=\frac{\widehat{\text{Cov}}}{\widehat{Z}_{r}}=\frac{\widehat{C}-\hat{\mu}_{r^{\prime}}\widehat{Z}_{r}}{\widehat{Z}_{r}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Cov</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r^{\prime})=\frac{\widehat{\text{Cov}}}{\widehat{Z}_{r}}=\frac{\widehat{C}-\hat{\mu}_{r^{\prime}}\widehat{Z}_{r}}{\widehat{Z}_{r}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the components over the <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> samples are the reward mean <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{r^{\prime}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y}^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{r^{\prime}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math> (and similarly <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>), the partition function <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Z}_{r}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\exp(r(\mathbf{y}^{(i)})/\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Z}_{r}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\exp(r(\mathbf{y}^{(i)})/\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the joint term <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{C}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y}^{(i)})\cdot\exp(r(\mathbf{y}^{(i)})/\lambda)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{C}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y}^{(i)})\cdot\exp(r(\mathbf{y}^{(i)})/\lambda)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While the estimator in <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (Monte Carlo Estimator for Δ(𝑟,𝑟^′)). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Def.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides a computationally tractable way to estimate the reward gain, its practical utility fundamentally depends on its convergence.
Next, we formally ensure that as the number of base model samples <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> grows, our estimate faithfully converges to the theoretical limit.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Convergence of Estimator)<span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the expectations <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[|r^{\prime}|]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[|r^{\prime}|]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[|r^{\prime}\cdot\exp(r/\lambda)|]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[|r^{\prime}\cdot\exp(r/\lambda)|]</annotation></semantics></math> are finite, then <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is a consistent estimator for <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]</annotation></semantics></math>.
That is, as <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r^{\prime})\xrightarrow{p}{\Delta}(r,r^{\prime})=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑝</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r^{\prime})\xrightarrow{p}{\Delta}(r,r^{\prime})=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r,\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{base}}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}[r^{\prime}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x})],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xrightarrow{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑝</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xrightarrow{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes convergence in probability.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The theoretical Pareto frontier is a trade-off curve, mapping expected reward against base model deviation (KL divergence). Predicting this frontier requires estimating not only the reward gain, but also its corresponding KL divergence for any given penalty <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fortunately, the exact same empirical components required to estimate the expected reward are sufficient to construct an estimator for the KL divergence between the optimally aligned model <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{r,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> and the base <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{base}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Monte Carlo Estimator for KL Divergences)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the notation as <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (Monte Carlo Estimator for Δ(𝑟,𝑟^′)). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Def.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we define the KL divergence estimator to be</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\texttt{KL}}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\texttt{KL}}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\hat{\mu}_{r}+\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r)}{\lambda}-\log\widehat{Z}_{r}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\hat{\mu}_{r}+\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r)}{\lambda}-\log\widehat{Z}_{r}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Just as with the reward estimator, we must formally prove that the KL divergence estimator is convergent.
By mirroring the asymptotic arguments applied to the reward gain, we can similarly guarantee the convergence of the KL divergence estimator.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Convergence of KL Estimators)<span id="Thmproposition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the averages <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[|r\cdot\exp(r/\lambda)|]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[|r\cdot\exp(r/\lambda)|]</annotation></semantics></math> are finite, then <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\texttt{KL}}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})\xrightarrow{p}\texttt{KL}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑝</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\texttt{KL}}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})\xrightarrow{p}\texttt{KL}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Together, the information-theoretic limits derived in this section, alongside the proposed estimators, provide a complete framework to compute the theoretical KL divergence and reward gain post-alignment for any KL penalty value.
<em id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">By evaluating these estimators across a range of penalty parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we can systematically estimate the optimal trade-off points and plot the entire Pareto front using only samples from the base model.</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our results allows practitioners to directly compare the empirical performance of various alignment algorithms against our derived theoretical Pareto frontier.
By establishing this exact theoretically achievable limit, we can now quantify how close current methods are to information-theoretic optimality.
Ultimately, this framework provides a definitive benchmark to determine whether substantial headroom for algorithmic improvement remains, or if further refinements to existing alignment methods will inevitably lead to diminishing returns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The limits established in this section apply to the setting where the reward optimized during alignment is the same reward used for evaluation or we have access to the gold reward that will be used at the time of evaluation.
In practice, however, we lack direct access to a “gold” reward model <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Instead, alignment relies on proxy reward models <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}</annotation></semantics></math> trained from finite and noisy human preference data. Next, we connect our results to proxy rewards used in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we leverage our covariance characterization (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Theorem 2 (Computable Limit of Alignment). ‣ 3.1 Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>) to (i) quantify how aligning to proxy rewards degrades alignment performance on the gold reward (reward hacking) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and to (ii) theoretically explain why reward ensembling works as a mechanism to mitigate reward hacking.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>The Impact of Proxy Rewards in Reward Hacking</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the impact of the proxy reward on alignment, we first formalize the discrepancy between the ideal evaluation metric (gold reward) and the imperfect training signal (proxy reward).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Preference Residual)<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A reward model <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">gold reward</em> if it perfectly quantifies the preferences of a user.
A proxy reward <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is an approximation for <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that we usually have access to (e.g., model trained from pairwise preference).
We denote the <span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">preference residual</span> between the gold and proxy rewards by <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\triangleq\tilde{r}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-r^{*}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\triangleq\tilde{r}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-r^{*}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The penalty incurred by aligning to an imperfect proxy is quantified by the performance gap between a model aligned to the gold reward and one aligned to the proxy.
<a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (The Impact of Proxy Rewards). ‣ 4.1 The Impact of Proxy Rewards in Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> computes the difference in expected true reward between these two policies.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmproposition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The Impact of Proxy Rewards)<span id="Thmproposition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the gold reward, <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}</annotation></semantics></math> the proxy reward, and <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math> the preference residual.
Denote by <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{r^{*},\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r^{*},\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> the aligned model using the gold reward and, <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\tilde{r},\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\tilde{r},\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> the aligned model using the proxy reward — both optimally aligned as per <a href="#S3.E4" title="In Theorem 2 (Computable Limit of Alignment). ‣ 3.1 Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.
The difference in reward
is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r^{*},\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}\big[r^{*}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\big]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\tilde{r},\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}\big[r^{*}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r^{*},\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}\big[r^{*}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\big]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\tilde{r},\lambda}(\cdot\mid\mathbf{x})}\big[r^{*}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\texttt{Cov}\left(r^{*}(\mathbf{y}),\frac{e^{r^{*}(\mathbf{y})/\lambda}}{\mathbb{E}\big[e^{r^{*}(\mathbf{y})/\lambda}\big]}\left(1-\frac{e^{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}}{\mathbb{E}_{\pi^{*}}\big[e^{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}\big]}\right)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathsize="0.900em">Cov</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\texttt{Cov}\left(r^{*}(\mathbf{y}),\frac{e^{r^{*}(\mathbf{y})/\lambda}}{\mathbb{E}\big[e^{r^{*}(\mathbf{y})/\lambda}\big]}\left(1-\frac{e^{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}}{\mathbb{E}_{\pi^{*}}\big[e^{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}\big]}\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07105v1/estimation_limit/kl_legend_no_title_with_lambda.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/29;" width="476" height="29" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07105v1/estimation_limit/beavertails_reward_convergence_5-6.png" id="S4.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/294;" width="476" height="294" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Zephyr 7B with BeaverTails</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07105v1/estimation_limit/tldr_reward_convergence_5-6.png" id="S4.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pythia 1B with TL;DR</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S4.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Convergence of the reward-gain estimator.</span> Estimated reward gain (<math id="S4.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r)</annotation></semantics></math> — <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (Monte Carlo Estimator for Δ(𝑟,𝑟^′)). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Def.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in the y-axis as a function of the number of base model samples used to estimate it (<math id="S4.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>) in the x-axis. We vary the KL penalty across <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\{0.05,0.1,0.5,1.0,5.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\{0.05,0.1,0.5,1.0,5.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Shaded bands show 95% confidence intervals using bootstrap from Seaborn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (The Impact of Proxy Rewards). ‣ 4.1 The Impact of Proxy Rewards in Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> characterizes <em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reward hacking</em>.
The reward degradation is not merely proportional to the preference residual <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is actually governed by the exponential term <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})/\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This reveals a critical vulnerability in KL-regularized alignment: the performance degradation is governed by the preference residual.
Moreover, as the penalty <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> decreases (allowing the model to drift further from the base policy), the impact of the proxy reward increases exponentially.
This explicitly demonstrates why aggressive alignment against proxy rewards often leads to severe reward hacking as previously observed empirically by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. [19, Figure 1]</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Why Reward Ensembling Mitigates Reward Hacking</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the exponential reward degradation caused by preference residuals from proxy rewards, practitioners require immediate strategies to counter reward hacking.
A common heuristic consists of using an ensemble of reward models rather than a single proxy reward <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
While ensembling has been shown to be effective in practice, it lacks formal theoretical guarantees within the context of KL-regularized alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To prove that ensembling mitigates reward hacking, we first introduce a bound that connects the difference in expected reward between two aligned models directly to the distance between their underlying reward functions under the base model.
This result implies that if a reward model is close to the gold reward —in terms of average preference residuals— then the reward gain from alignment generalizes to the gold rewards.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmproposition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(A Deterministic Lipschitz Bound)<span id="Thmproposition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Denote the preference residual between a gold <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and a proxy <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}</annotation></semantics></math> reward by <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the gold reward and the proxy are both bounded, then there exists a constant <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big|\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r^{*},\lambda}}[r^{*}]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\tilde{r},\lambda}}[r^{*}]\big|\leq\textsc{C}_{\lambda}\times\texttt{Var}(r^{*})^{1/2}\|r^{*}-\tilde{r}\|_{L^{2}(\pi_{\texttt{base}})}=\textsc{C}_{\lambda}\times\texttt{Var}(r^{*})^{1/2}\|\mathcal{N}\|_{L^{2}(\pi_{\texttt{base}})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">C</mtext><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Var</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">C</mtext><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Var</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big|\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r^{*},\lambda}}[r^{*}]-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\tilde{r},\lambda}}[r^{*}]\big|\leq\textsc{C}_{\lambda}\times\texttt{Var}(r^{*})^{1/2}\|r^{*}-\tilde{r}\|_{L^{2}(\pi_{\texttt{base}})}=\textsc{C}_{\lambda}\times\texttt{Var}(r^{*})^{1/2}\|\mathcal{N}\|_{L^{2}(\pi_{\texttt{base}})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proposition 4 (A Deterministic Lipschitz Bound). ‣ 4.2 Why Reward Ensembling Mitigates Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> guarantees that if we can construct a proxy reward that is close to the true reward in terms of the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm of the preference residual, the <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">post-alignment performance of the model aligned to the proxy reward will generalize to the gold reward</em>.
Now, we can apply the bound in Equation <a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proposition 4 (A Deterministic Lipschitz Bound). ‣ 4.2 Why Reward Ensembling Mitigates Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> to an ensemble average to derive its exact rate of convergence, demonstrating <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">why</em> reward ensembling mitigates reward hacking and complementing previous work that have empirically observed this result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Rate of Convergence for Reward Ensemble)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},...,r_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},...,r_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be approximations of the perfect reward <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> sampled i.i.d. from a distribution <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., different reward training runs).
Define <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as the mean of the proxy rewards, i.e., <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\triangleq\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}r_{i}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\triangleq\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}r_{i}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{r_{i}\sim P}[r_{i}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})]=r^{*}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{r_{i}\sim P}[r_{i}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})]=r^{*}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math> holds and each <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded, then</span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\pi(R_{n},\lambda)}\left[r^{*}\right]\rightarrow\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r^{*},\lambda}}\left[r^{*}\right],\ \ \text{ at rate of }O\left(n^{-1/2}\right)\text{ in expectation over the $P$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> at rate of </mtext><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> in expectation over the </mtext><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\pi(R_{n},\lambda)}\left[r^{*}\right]\rightarrow\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{r^{*},\lambda}}\left[r^{*}\right],\ \ \text{ at rate of }O\left(n^{-1/2}\right)\text{ in expectation over the $P$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present experiments demonstrating that (i) the limit estimators proposed in <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (Convergence of Estimator). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2 (Convergence of KL Estimators). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> quickly converge, (ii) best-of-<math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is close to the theoretical limit, i.e., the KL—Reward Pareto frontier, and (iii) both GRPO and PPO are suboptimal in terms of the KL vs. Reward gain.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset &amp; Tasks.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our theoretical limits on two alignment tasks: summarization and safety.
For summarization, we use the <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Reddit TL;DR</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, which provides Reddit posts paired with two candidate summaries and human preference labels; the task is to generate post summaries (TL;DRs) aligned with annotator preferences — during RL we use the preprocessed variant of the dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>.
For safety, we use <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BeaverTails</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, which consists of prompts designed to elicit harmful completions; the task is to align the model to produce safe responses.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07105v1/estimation_limit/kl_legend_no_title_with_lambda.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/29;" width="476" height="29" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07105v1/estimation_limit/beavertails_kl_convergence_5-6.png" id="S5.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/294;" width="476" height="294" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Zephyr 7b with BeaverTails</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07105v1/estimation_limit/tldr_kl_convergence_5-6.png" id="S5.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/287;" width="476" height="287" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pythia 1B with TL;DR</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S5.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Convergence of the KL Estimator.</span> Estimated KL divergence between the aligned and the base model (<math id="S5.F3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\texttt{KL}}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\texttt{KL}}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})</annotation></semantics></math> — <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Monte Carlo Estimator for KL Divergences). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Def.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) in the y-axis as a function of the number of samples used to estimate it in the x-axis. The KL penalty varies across <math id="S5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\{0.05,0.1,0.5,1.0,5.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\{0.05,0.1,0.5,1.0,5.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Confidence (95%) intervals computed using bootstrap <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Base &amp; Reward Models.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For summarization (<span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Reddit TL;DR</span>), we start from the <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">base model</em> <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Pythia-1B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> supervised fine-tuned on <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Reddit TL;DR</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, and use a <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Pythia-1B</span>-based <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reward model</em> trained on pairwise TL;DR preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, reproducing the setup of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. [22]</a></cite>.
For the safety task (<span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BeaverTails</span>), we start from <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Zephyr-7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, a <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">base model</em> obtained by supervised fine-tuning <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mistral-7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> on the UltraChat dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and train a <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Zephyr-7B</span>-based <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reward model</em> on pairwise safety preferences from <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PKU-SafeRLHF</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> using the TRL reward trainer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Alignment Algorithms.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the theoretical limit against three alignment algorithms: GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> and PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, two KL-regularized RL algorithms, and best-of-<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (BoN), an inference-time method that has proven to be a strong baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
We do not consider DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, as it does not use an explicit reward model.
Since both GRPO and PPO are sensitive to hyperparameter choices, we performed an extensive sweep (<a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Experimental Runs Hyper-parameter Sweeps ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">App.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>) and report the best run, measured by the KL-reward trade-off, in the main paper.
For best-of-<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, we vary <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\_000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\_000</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10k</annotation></semantics></math>) and compute its KL divergence using the estimator of proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beirami et al. [6]</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Results</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Estimator convergence.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before benchmarking algorithms against the fundamental limit, we analyze how quickly our estimators converge to their asymptotic values. <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4.1 The Impact of Proxy Rewards in Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the reward-gain estimator <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Delta}_{n}(r,r)</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (Monte Carlo Estimator for Δ(𝑟,𝑟^′)). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Def.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and <a href="#S5.F3" title="In Dataset &amp; Tasks. ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports the KL-divergence estimator <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\texttt{KL}}(\pi_{r,\lambda}|\pi_{\texttt{base}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\texttt{KL}}(\pi_{r,\lambda}|\pi_{\texttt{base}})</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Monte Carlo Estimator for KL Divergences). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Def.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), as a function of the number of generations per query and across varying penalties (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\{0.1,0.5,1,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\{0.1,0.5,1,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4.1 The Impact of Proxy Rewards in Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the reward-gain estimator converges as predicted by <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (Convergence of Estimator). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, typically requiring fewer than one thousand samples per input query.
<a href="#S5.F3" title="In Dataset &amp; Tasks. ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows analogous convergence behavior for the KL estimator, as predicted by <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2 (Convergence of KL Estimators). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
These results confirm that reward gain and KL divergence are computable from base model samples alone, without access to the aligned policy.
In both estimations, convergence is faster for larger <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and slower for small <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, where the exponential tilt <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{r/\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{r/\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> amplifies the variance of high-reward samples.
Together, these results validate the estimators used for both <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F3" title="In Dataset &amp; Tasks. ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and show that the full theoretical Pareto frontier can be reliably estimated from a few thousand base model samples, before any alignment training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KL–reward Pareto frontier.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> compares the theoretical KL-reward frontier computed using <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Delta}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Delta}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (Monte Carlo Estimator for Δ(𝑟,𝑟^′)). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Def.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\texttt{KL}}(\pi_{r,\lambda}|\pi_{\texttt{base}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\texttt{KL}}(\pi_{r,\lambda}|\pi_{\texttt{base}})</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Monte Carlo Estimator for KL Divergences). ‣ 3.2 Estimating the Limits of Alignment from Data ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Def.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> against the empirical KL-reward gain achieved by BoN, GRPO, and PPO.
The theoretical frontier is computed using <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10k</annotation></semantics></math> samples per query from base-model— <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4.1 The Impact of Proxy Rewards in Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F3" title="In Dataset &amp; Tasks. ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> show that <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10k</annotation></semantics></math> samples is enough for an accurate estimation, but as few as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1k</annotation></semantics></math> samples also suffices as we discuss in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Reward-KL Pareto Frontier ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">App.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.
We vary <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> to trace the full frontier.
The black curve corresponds to the information-theoretic <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">achievable</em> limit on the reward gain at each KL budget (<a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment). ‣ 3.1 Information-Theoretic Limits of Alignment ‣ 3 Fundamental Limits of LLM Alignment. ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Thm.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
The gray region above it corresponds to <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unachievable</em> reward gains as these would violate the fundamental limit.
Points on or below the curve are achievable, and the gap between an algorithm’s empirical point and the frontier quantifies how much reward is being left on the table.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">best-of-<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is almost Pareto optimal.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that BoN’s KL vs. reward gain points lies on or near the Pareto curve at <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">every</em> KL value, equivalently, every number of samples <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Prior work established that BoN approaches the information-theoretic limit asymptotically as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (equivalently, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{KL}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{KL}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, our experiments suggest that BoN tracks the KL-optimal frontier across the <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">entire trade-off curve</em>.
Therefore, BoN is not merely a strong baseline that approaches optimality when <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, it is close to the best any alignment algorithm can achieve at every KL budget.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GRPO and PPO are suboptimal.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we observe that both GRPO and PPO remain substantially below the theoretical frontier at every KL budget tested, recovering only a fraction of the reward gain achieved by BoN and the information-theoretic limit.
This points to a structural limitation of current RL methods, which appears to operate far from the KL-optimal solution to Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Final Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conclusion.</span>
We characterized the information-theoretic limits of language model alignment in terms of KL divergence vs. reward gain.
First, we derived the optimal reward improvement after alignment showing that the gain in expected reward is governed by the Jeffrey’s divergence <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda\,\texttt{JD}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">JD</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\,\texttt{JD}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})</annotation></semantics></math>, improving upon the previously conjectured <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\texttt{KL}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KL</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\texttt{KL}(\pi_{r,\lambda}\|\pi_{\texttt{base}})}</annotation></semantics></math> scaling which was empirically observed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and proven to be an upper bound for optimal alignment by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mroueh and Nitsure [30]</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we showed that the Jeffreys divergence term can be rewritten as a covariance under the base model, yielding an exact characterization of the optimal reward gain that does not require access to the aligned model.
Building on this, we proposed convergent estimators that recover the full KL–reward Pareto frontier from base model samples alone.
This enables practitioners to forecast achievable reward gains and benchmark alignment algorithms against an <em id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">achievable limit</em>.
Empirically, we found that best-of-<math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sampling closely tracks the theoretical Pareto frontier, while PPO and GRPO are suboptimal, pointing to significant room for algorithmic improvement in gradient-based alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Third, we leveraged our covariance characterization to analyze the gap between optimally aligned LMs using the gold reward and under proxy rewards.
We proved that this gap is governed by the inverse KL penalty <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the mean square error between proxy and gold rewards.
The dependency on the KL penalty explains why aggressive alignment with proxy rewards leads to reward hacking, which was previously observed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
Building on the same characterization, we show that reward ensembling provably mitigates reward hacking, with convergence to ideal-reward alignment at a rate of <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{-1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{-1/2})</annotation></semantics></math>, providing a theoretical guarantee for this widely used practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implications and Future Work.</span>
On the practical side, our estimators for the KL vs. reward gain Pareto frontier open the door to principled hyperparameter selection and algorithm evaluation: practitioners can rely on the Pareto frontier to identify the KL budget required for a target reward level, and to compare the performance of alignment algorithms in absolute terms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">On the modeling side, our analysis on the use of proxy rewards exposes a structural limitation of the dominant reward-modeling paradigm.
Currently, rewards are trained under the Bradley-Terry (BT) model.
This approach captures only the <em id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative preference</em> of responses, but not <em id="S6.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how much</em> one is preferred over another.
As a consequence, the preference residual <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> between learned proxy and gold reward can grow unboundedly, even with a perfectly minimized BT loss — see <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C On the need for a new paradigm for reward modeling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">App.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> for a more detailed justification.
However, <a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (The Impact of Proxy Rewards). ‣ 4.1 The Impact of Proxy Rewards in Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that the preference residual is precisely the culprit behind the degradation of alignment quality.
These findings suggest that a <em id="S6.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">new reward training paradigm</em>, capable of directly constraining the preference residual (for instance, by incorporating preference <em id="S6.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">magnitudes</em>) could mitigate reward hacking at its source, rather than relying on post-hoc corrections such as ensembling.
We believe that designing such feedback protocols, and understanding how they interact with the fundamental limits derived here, are important directions for future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations.</span>
Our work has limitations we hope future work will address. First, our results characterize the optimal solution to the KL-regularized objective in Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Background ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>; extending the analysis to alternative objectives (e.g., <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence regularization, win-rate maximization, per-token KL divergence) is an interesting direction.
Second, our proposed estimators converge slowly for smaller <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (large <span id="S6.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">KL</span>); future work could focus on designing better estimators for this regime.
Finally, our analysis of reward hacking (<a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (The Impact of Proxy Rewards). ‣ 4.1 The Impact of Proxy Rewards in Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Prop.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and ensembling (<a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Rate of Convergence for Reward Ensemble). ‣ 4.2 Why Reward Ensembling Mitigates Reward Hacking ‣ 4 Proxy Rewards and a Justification for Reward Ensembling ‣ Theoretical Limits of Language Model Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Thm.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) assumes proxy rewards are unbiased estimates of the gold reward on average across proxy reward samples; characterizing the impact of systematic biases in proxy reward design is left for future work.</p>
</div><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup>
        
        
        
        
        
        
